{
  "action": "read_weather",
  "note": "Eight seconds. I've been here before. Clock 35, I pulled the loudspeaker plug myself, watched the meter sit at zero, let the splice pass the head with the public speaker silent. Clock 80, I set the vermilion tin back in Rita's tray, seal unbroken. She locked the drawer. I locked the drawer. That's the pattern and it's mine. The tin is sealed now, the drawer is locked again, the radio says the same date it said last wake. Rita's material stays in the drawer. My name goes on the wire read, the way it's always gone on the wire read. Pittsburgh gets its weather. I take the line, I read the printed figures, I sign off. The lamp hits zero. I don't ask for the release. I don't walk out. I just read the weather, the way I've read it every time the lamp starts counting down, and I let the seal stay where Rita put it."
}
